    "case_id": "175",
    "case_summary": "A 9-year-old boy presented with a 3-month history of a painless swelling in the right upper posterior tooth region that had grown progressively, causing facial asymmetry. His medical and family histories were unremarkable. Examination revealed a 4.0×3.5 cm diffuse, well-defined, hard, nontender swelling on the right middle third of the face, extending from 1 cm below the infraorbital margin to the inferior border of the mandible and from the ala of the nose posteriorly to the anterior border of the ramus. The overlying skin was normal. Intraorally, there was a 4.0×3.0 cm ovoid, hard, nontender swelling in the right buccal vestibule with buccal cortical expansion and slight palatal expansion but no mucosal changes. A provisional clinical diagnosis of juvenile ossifying fibroma was made, with fibrous dysplasia as a consideration. \n\nPanoramic and periapical radiographs showed a ground-glass trabecular pattern with radiolucency around the roots of deciduous teeth and erupting premolars. A maxillary occlusal film demonstrated increased radiopacity, buccal cortical expansion, and a ground-glass appearance without palatal involvement. Cone-beam CT revealed expansion of the buccal and palatal cortical plates, involvement of more than half of the right maxillary sinus, a well-defined margin, and a ground-glass internal pattern. Based on these images, a radiological diagnosis of juvenile ossifying fibroma of the right posterior maxilla was made, with fibrous dysplasia considered less likely because “the margin of the swelling was well-defined and well demarcated from the surrounding areas which was very much unlike FD whose margin is ill-defined and merges with the surrounding areas.”\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Fibrous dysplasia\nB. A clinical diagnosis of juvenile ossifying fibroma was made because “a provisional diagnosis of juvenile ossifying\nC. Fibrous dysplasia was included in the initial differential diagnosis\nD. Radiologically, juvenile ossifying fibroma was favored as “based on the radiological findings, a radiological diagnosis\nE. Fibrous dysplasia was considered but deprioritized on imaging since “the margin of the swelling was well\nF. Histopathology ultimately confirmed fibrous dysplasia\nG. None\nH. None\n",
